## Why This Is Asked
This checks understanding of test isolation and determinism in E2E tests using mocks for external services.

## Key Concepts
- Data seeding and reset for repeatable tests
- Network interception and mocked APIs
- End-to-end flow verification and backend validation
- CI reliability and speed trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright test sketch
test('checkout end-to-end', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/payments', route => route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ status:'success', id:'PAY123' }) }));
  await page.request.post('https://test.api/reset-db', { data: { seed: 'default' } });
  await page.goto('https://shop.example.com/product/1');
  await page.click('text=Add to cart');
  await page.goto('https://shop.example.com/checkout');
  await page.fill('#name', 'Test User');
  await page.fill('#address', '123 Main St');
  await page.click('text=Pay');
  await page.locator('text=Order Confirmed').waitFor();
  // backend verification
  const res = await page.request.get('https://test.api/orders/123');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this test for a flaky payment API?
- What metrics would you collect to monitor CI stability for this flow?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests involving background work and eventual UI changes are common in data apps. This question checks understanding of test isolation, idempotency, and end-to-end timing without flakiness.

## Key Concepts

- Background job queues and completion signaling
- API and UI mocking for determinism
- Data seeding and range validation
- Test isolation and deterministic timing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright pseudo-example
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('export enqueues, completes, and provides download', async ({ page }) => {
  await seedDataForRange('2023-01-01','2023-01-31');
  await page.goto('/export');
  await page.fill('#start', '2023-01-01');
  await page.fill('#end', '2023-01-31');
  await page.click('#submit-export');
  await expect(page.locator('#progress')).toHaveText(/in progress/);
  await mockDelay(10000); // simulate 10s processing
  await page.waitForSelector('#download-link', { timeout: 20000 });
  const csv = await page.locator('#download-link').evaluate(node => fetch(node.href).then(r => r.text()));
  expect(csv).toContain('2023-01-01,2023-01-31');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this test to handle export failures and retry logic?
- What patterns help keep tests fast while still validating end-to-end behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
Probes ability to design robust E2E tests for streaming pipelines, focusing on exactly-once semantics, replay safety, fault injection, and cross-store validation.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming tests across Kafka, storage, and materialized views
- Exactly-once and idempotent writes
- Replay/failure scenarios and broker partition events
- Tenant data isolation and deterministic mocks
- Observability: SLA validation and latency metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo end-to-end test flow for streaming ingestion
async function testStreamingPipeline() {
  const tenantId = uniqueTenant();
  seedTenant(tenantId);
  produceKafkaEvents(tenantId, 10000);
  waitForSink(tenantId);
  replaySubset(tenantId, 1000);
  verifyNoDuplicates(tenantId);
  simulateBrokerFailure();
  validateEventualConsistency(tenantId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate idempotency across multiple partitions?
- Which metrics and alarms would you configure for failure scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests must cover internationalization, accessibility, and end-to-end flow in a single system. This question probes test design for localization, RTL, and deterministic reproducibility across many locales.

## Key Concepts
- Locale/RTL handling, i18n data, currency/date formatting
- E2E test parametrization, deterministic seeds, mocks
- Accessibility tree verification in dynamic UIs
- Network throttling and flakiness handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright pseudo-test outline for locale switch
test('e2e locale switch', async ({ page, browserRoom }) => {
  const ctx = await browser.newContext({ locale: 'en-US' });
  const p = page;
  await p.goto('/');
  // switch locale mid-session
  await p.click('#locale-switch');
  await p.click('text=العربية');
  // continue flow
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate locale data seeds per locale to avoid cross-test contamination?
- How would you validate that translations load within a 2-second window in a flaky network scenario?

